Finding: {"title":"Row-Level Security tenant isolation is fully deployed at the DB but never activated by application code","severity":"CRITICAL","area":"Multi-tenant data isolation (ADR-002)","files":["src/lib/db-tenant-context.ts","drizzle/0144_enable_row_level_security.sql"],"detail":"Migration 0144 enables RLS with tenant_isolation/tenant_insert policies on ~80 tenant-scoped tables, gated on current_setting('app.flight_school_id') via get_current_flight_school_id(). The only application code that ever calls set_config('app.flight_school_id', ...) is setTenantContext() in src/lib/db-tenant-context.ts — and a repo-wide grep shows setTenantContext/clearTenantContext have zero callers anywhere in src/. Every real query therefore executes with app.flight_school_id unset (NULL). Cross-checked routes-stripe.ts: tenant scoping there is done entirely via explicit flightSchoolId !== callerOrgId checks in application code, not RLS.","impact":"The migration comment and function docstrings assert RLS is 'the mechanism that prevents cross-org data leakage,' but it is not wired up. Any application-code bug that omits a WHERE flight_school_id = ... filter has no DB-level backstop. This is exactly the class of incident ADR-002 exists to prevent, and the safety net is currently a no-op.","recommendation":"Either (1) wire setTenantContext/clearTenantContext into the actual request path (Hono middleware, per-request, before any query) and verify the connection pooling model actually preserves session-local GUCs per logical request (see next finding), or (2) if RLS is intentionally not used and app-layer WHERE clauses are the real control, remove/document the RLS migration and dead functions so nobody trusts a phantom backstop.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

